The bug is in:

`org.joda.time.format.DateTimeParserBucket.computeMillis(boolean, java.lang.String)`

Diagnosis:
- The failing tests all involve `parseInto(...)` where the target `MutableDateTime` already has a year (2004), but the parsed text supplies only month or month+day.
- Actual results show the year being overwritten with the formatter’s default year:
  - usually `2000`
  - or `2012` when `withDefaultYear(2012)` is used
- Static inspection shows why:

```java
if (count > 0) {
    // alter base year for parsing if first field is month or day
    DurationField months = DurationFieldType.months().getField(iChrono);
    DurationField days = DurationFieldType.days().getField(iChrono);
    DurationField first = savedFields[0].iField.getDurationField();
    if (compareReverse(first, months) >= 0 && compareReverse(first, days) <= 0) {
        saveField(DateTimeFieldType.year(), iDefaultYear);
        return computeMillis(resetFields, text);
    }
}
```

This logic unconditionally injects `year = iDefaultYear` whenever the parsed fields start at month/day granularity. But in `parseInto`, the bucket is initialized from the existing instant (`iMillis = instantLocal`), so missing fields should normally be preserved from that base instant. Instead, `computeMillis` replaces the existing year with the default year.

That exactly matches the failures:
- parsing `"5"` into a 2004 date becomes year 2000, month 5
- parsing `"2 29"` becomes 2000-02-29, or 2012-02-29 if custom default year is set

So the root cause is that `computeMillis` applies the default year even during `parseInto` against an existing instant, when it should preserve the base instant’s year. The method is using the default year too aggressively.

